   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Data Preprocessing"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b626c1d4",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Data Loading"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "id": "b35a2a3b",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Data Shape: (9999, 784)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "import pandas as pd\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import math as m\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "\n",
    "# Load the Dataset and Check Shape\n",
    "df = pd.read_csv(\"csvImages 10k x 784.csv\")\n",
    "df_clean = df.copy()\n",
    "print(\"Data Shape:\", df_clean.shape)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "e29d8205",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "The data was obtained from Kaggle, and downloaded form [Learn](https://learn.rrc.ca/d2l/le/content/759307/Home). It was said to contain 10,000 images of size 28x28 (784 pixels), with each image being flattened as a 1D-array. The individual values were said to be recorded as grayscale so we expected there to be exactly 784 columns. Upon inspecting the shape of the data, we can confirm that there are 784 columns, coincidign with our assumptions of it being grayscale and will proceed accordingly. Of the 10,000 rows, we found that one was missing. As we still have enough data for training our encoder, this will not cause any problems going forward."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "176e83a8",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Check Data Integrity"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "id": "3873ab72",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Min value across all columns: 0\n",
      "Max value across all columns: 255\n",
      "Number of Duplicate Rows: 1\n",
      "Number of missing values in data: 0\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "print(\"Min value across all columns:\",df_clean.min().min())\n",
    "print(\"Max value across all columns:\",df_clean.max().max())\n",
    "print(\"Number of Duplicate Rows:\",df_clean.duplicated().sum())\n",
    "print(\"Number of missing values in data:\",df_clean.isna().sum().sum())"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "fbf9c43a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Since our data is supposed to contain pixel data for grayscale images, the values should naturally fall within the range of [0,255]. To check this, we checked the minimum and maximum values in each column, and then took the maximum and minimum value from this set. This gives us an accurate representation for our value ranges as no column will have a value larger than the absolute max or lower than the absolute min. As we had expected, the data across all columns did fall within the range of [0,255], so we can determine that there are no unexpected grayscale values.  \n",
    "Next, we checked whether any images had been duplicated. Images with the same exact pixel information could cause our encoder to disproportionately value them and overadjust it's weights. Since only 2 rows contained the same pixel data, we will be removing one of them to preserve the quality of our data.  \n",
    "Finally, we checked if any column contained missing values. Since this also returned no missing pixel values across all the images recorded in the data, we can continue with the dataset without further cleaning.  "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "id": "e380ed90",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Cleaned Data Shape: (9998, 784)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "df_clean = df_clean.drop_duplicates()\n",
    "print(\"Cleaned Data Shape:\", df_clean.shape)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "52cc0f4d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Reshape for Visualizations"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "id": "b3140f75",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Shape of Array: (9998, 28, 28)\n",
      "Shape of Each Row: (28, 28)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Convert DataFrame to Numpy Array and Normalize Values\n",
    "img_data = df_clean.values/255\n",
    "img_data = img_data.reshape((-1,28,28))\n",
    "print(\"Shape of Array:\",img_data.shape)\n",
    "print(\"Shape of Each Row:\",img_data[0].shape)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "dfcd0031",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "After checking the data, we converted the DataFrame into a NumPy array and divided every pixel by 255. This changes the values from 0–255 to 0–1, so every pixel is on the same scale. This matters because PCA and clustering are based on distances.  \n",
    "Next, we reshaped each row of 784 values into a 28 × 28 image so we could display the images with Matplotlib."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "00e4e7b0",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Pre-Encoding Visualizations"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "id": "a92b53ec",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1600x1000 with 5 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "plt.figure(figsize=(16,10))                   \n",
    "for i in range(5):\n",
    "    plt.subplot(1,5,i+1)                                \n",
    "    plt.imshow(img_data[i].reshape(28,28),cmap=\"gray\")   \n",
    "    plt.axis(\"off\")                                    \n",
    "plt.show()                                               "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c6d15217",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We plotted the first five images using using a grayscale colormap. These original images serve as the baseline we will compare the decoded images against when judging the autoencoder.  \n",
    "Each image shows a light digit stroke on a dark background, roughly centred in the frame. Most pixels, especially around the edges, are 0 (black) and carry no information about the digit.  \n",
    "Much of each image is \"empty\" space, which suggests the images can be compressed substantially without losing the digit itself."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "749f72ac",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Reshape for Data Encoding"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 6,
   "id": "bdee7839",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Shape of Array: (9998, 784)\n",
      "Shape of Each Row: (784,)\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "img_data = img_data.reshape((-1,28**2))\n",
    "print(\"Shape of Array:\",img_data.shape)\n",
    "print(\"Shape of Each Row:\",img_data[0].shape)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "374c5821",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We flattened each 28×28 image back into a one-dimensional array of 784 values. Keras `Dense` layers take one-dimensional input, so the autoencoder's input layer expects 784 values per image.  \n",
    "The data shape is back to **(9998, 784)**, with values between 0 and 1, which is ready to feed into the network. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "f48a61fc",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Clustering Section"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 7,
   "id": "87b3683b-f207-4bd8-b803-55f501b2c3b3",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import inspect                                    \n",
    "import time                                       \n",
    "from sklearn.decomposition import PCA             \n",
    "from sklearn.cluster import KMeans, AgglomerativeClustering, DBSCAN   \n",
    "from sklearn.metrics import silhouette_score    \n",
    "from sklearn.neighbors import NearestNeighbors    \n",
    "from sklearn.manifold import TSNE                \n",
    "import matplotlib.patheffects as pe               \n",
    "\n",
    "np.random.seed(0)                                "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "de800b47-a11d-4d4f-be62-50894e6bc23e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Reduce the data with PCA (n_components = 40)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 8,
   "id": "04b4fa68-926a-45ee-bf86-43f04e133760",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Shape before PCA: (9998, 784)\n",
      "Shape after PCA:  (9998, 40)\n",
      "Share of total variance kept by 40 components: 0.765\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "model_pca = PCA(n_components=40, random_state=0)\n",
    "img_pca = model_pca.fit_transform(img_data)\n",
    "print(\"Shape before PCA:\", img_data.shape) \n",
    "print(\"Shape after PCA: \", img_pca.shape)          \n",
    "print(\"Share of total variance kept by 40 components: \"f\"{model_pca.explained_variance_ratio_.sum():.3f}\")   "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 9,
   "id": "01a83050-c11b-4486-ad99-138e65fbae21",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Components needed for 70% of variance: 29\n",
      "Components needed for 80% of variance: 49\n",
      "Components needed for 90% of variance: 99\n",
      "Components needed for 95% of variance: 167\n"
     ]
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1000x500 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "pca_full = PCA(random_state=0).fit(img_data)                  \n",
    "cum_var = np.cumsum(pca_full.explained_variance_ratio_)       \n",
    "\n",
    "for target in [0.70, 0.80, 0.90, 0.95]:                       \n",
    "    n_needed = np.argmax(cum_var >= target) + 1               \n",
    "    print(f\"Components needed for {target:.0%} of variance: {n_needed}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5)) \n",
    "plt.plot(np.arange(1, len(cum_var) + 1), cum_var)             \n",
    "plt.axvline(40, color='red', linestyle='--', label='40 components (used)') \n",
    "plt.axhline(cum_var[39], color='grey', linestyle=':')\n",
    "plt.xlim(0, 784)                                     \n",
    "plt.xlabel('Number of principal components')         \n",
    "plt.ylabel('Cumulative explained variance')          \n",
    "plt.title('Cumulative Explained Variance of PCA on the Arabic Digit Images') \n",
    "plt.legend()                                                 \n",
    "plt.show()                                             "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "55f9244e-fb02-44bb-b148-712b0ec6c2af",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Reconstruct the images with PCA"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 10,
   "id": "d7f97acc-e335-490f-be77-1388d7436f96",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 1600x700 with 10 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "img_recon = model_pca.inverse_transform(img_pca)              \n",
    "\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(16, 7))                                   \n",
    "for i in range(5):                                            \n",
    "    plt.subplot(2, 5, i + 1)                                  \n",
    "    plt.imshow(img_data[i].reshape(28, 28).T, cmap='gray')\n",
    "    plt.title('Original'); plt.axis('off')                    \n",
    "    plt.subplot(2, 5, i + 6)                                  \n",
    "    plt.imshow(img_recon[i].reshape(28, 28).T, cmap='gray')   \n",
    "    plt.title('40 Principal Components'); plt.axis('off')          \n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "68ecb5b0-d23e-45be-934d-abeae450e65b",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**PCA results**\n",
    "\n",
    "We used PCA to shrink each image from 784 values down to 40. The 40 components keep about **76.5%** of the total variance in the data. The variance curve shows that keeping more components gives smaller and smaller gains. For example, it would take 49 components to reach 80% and 167 to reach 95%.\n",
    "\n",
    "In the reconstructed images, every digit is still easy to recognise from just 40 numbers. Only some fine detail around the edges of the strokes is lost. This tells us that 40 components keep the shape of the digit, which is the part that matters for clustering."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "cb34639c-02c1-4464-aaa8-a79b15fecb5c",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "source": [
    "### Models\n",
    "\n",
    "We tried 13 models in total: 4 K-Means, 3 hierarchical and 6 DBSCAN. To score each model the same way, we wrote a helper function called `evaluate`. It fits the model, calculates the silhouette score and saves the results to a table.\n",
    "\n",
    "We also recorded two extra things for each model, because the silhouette score on its own can be misleading:\n",
    "- **Noise %**: DBSCAN can throw points away as \"noise\", and the silhouette score is only calculated on the points it keeps.\n",
    "- **Largest cluster %**: if one cluster holds most of the images, the model is not really separating the digits."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 11,
   "id": "2081bfa3-1b4c-4fad-903e-2e9b44c8f901",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "results = []         \n",
    "cluster_labels = {}  \n",
    "modeling_results = dict()\n",
    "\n",
    "def evaluate(name, model, params):\n",
    "    start = time.time()                                   \n",
    "    labels = model.fit_predict(img_pca)                   \n",
    "    seconds = time.time() - start                        \n",
    "    clustered = labels != -1                              \n",
    "    n_clusters = len(np.unique(labels[clustered]))\n",
    "    noise_pct = 100 * (~clustered).mean()\n",
    "\n",
    "    if n_clusters > 1:                                    \n",
    "        sil = silhouette_score(img_pca[clustered], labels[clustered])   \n",
    "    else:\n",
    "        sil = np.nan                                     \n",
    "\n",
    "    sizes = np.bincount(labels[clustered]) if n_clusters > 0 else np.array([0])  \n",
    "    largest_pct = 100 * sizes.max() / len(labels)        \n",
    "\n",
    "    model_results = {'Model': name,\n",
    "                    'Hyperparameters': params,\n",
    "                    'Clusters': n_clusters,\n",
    "                    'Noise %': round(noise_pct, 1),\n",
    "                    'Largest cluster %': round(largest_pct, 1),\n",
    "                    'Silhouette': round(sil, 3),\n",
    "                    'Fit time (s)': round(seconds, 1),\n",
    "                    \"Labels\": labels,}\n",
    "    results.append(model_results)\n",
    "    cluster_labels[name] = labels\n",
    "    print(f\"{name:<28} clusters={n_clusters:<3} noise={noise_pct:5.1f}% \"f\"largest={largest_pct:5.1f}%  silhouette={sil:.3f}\")\n",
    "    return model_results"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7545bfd8-62ec-4cb9-9200-03f512ae00d9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### K- Means "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 12,
   "id": "1efec207-79e4-4680-bb83-fc986ffe695b",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "K-Means (k=8)                clusters=8   noise=  0.0% largest= 22.7%  silhouette=0.116\n",
      "K-Means (k=10)               clusters=10  noise=  0.0% largest= 21.0%  silhouette=0.122\n",
      "K-Means (k=12)               clusters=12  noise=  0.0% largest= 12.9%  silhouette=0.132\n",
      "K-Means (k=15)               clusters=15  noise=  0.0% largest= 11.6%  silhouette=0.133\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "i = 0\n",
    "for k in [8, 10, 12, 15]:                                           \n",
    "    km = KMeans(n_clusters=k,init='k-means++',n_init=10,random_state=0)                                    \n",
    "    modeling_results[i] = evaluate(f'K-Means (k={k})', km, f'n_clusters={k}, n_init=10')\n",
    "    i += 1"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "40b9d75a-92cd-4c34-9f96-f2fe0e99d853",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We tried K-Means with 8, 10, 12 and 15 clusters. We started with 10 because there are 10 digits, and tried the others to see whether fewer or more clusters fit the data better. All four models used k-means++ to pick the starting centres and n_init = 10, which runs the model 10 times and keeps the best result.\n",
    "\n",
    "The silhouette score went up slowly as we added clusters, from 0.116 (k = 8) to 0.133 (k = 15). The k = 10 model had a largest cluster holding 21% of the images."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "b271896e-e213-4472-a770-0c611490db07",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Hierachical Clustering"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 13,
   "id": "634699bc-b16e-41bb-bdd7-f2fdae0ed11c",
   "metadata": {
    "tags": []
   },
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Hierarchical (ward)          clusters=10  noise=  0.0% largest= 19.3%  silhouette=0.109\n",
      "Hierarchical (complete)      clusters=10  noise=  0.0% largest= 42.5%  silhouette=0.088\n",
      "Hierarchical (average)       clusters=10  noise=  0.0% largest= 81.0%  silhouette=0.140\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "for link in ['ward', 'complete', 'average']:                  \n",
    "    agg = AgglomerativeClustering(n_clusters=10,linkage=link)\n",
    "    modeling_results[i] = evaluate(f'Hierarchical ({link})', agg, f'n_clusters=10, linkage={link}')\n",
    "    i += 1"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2f21abc6-bf79-4c89-b309-106bb8fc06d9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Average linkage got the highest score (0.140), but it put 81% of the images into one giant cluster. Complete linkage put 42.5% into one cluster. Ward was the only one with balanced clusters (largest 19.3%), even though its score was lower (0.109)."
   ]
  },
