   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "78c274a5-e6a8-44a1-b825-70c6e8179c0e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "We decided to check what digits are within each cluster starting K-Means with k = 10, since there are 10 digits. When we checked its clusters, we found that it put most of the 3s and 4s together into one cluster, which is why its largest cluster was so big. It also put a lot of 3s in with the 7s and split the 0s across two clusters.\n",
    "\n",
    "Then we went back and tried k = 12. With 12 clusters, the 3s and 4s each get their own cluster, and so do the 1s, 2s, 5s, 7s and 8s. The two extra clusters went to digit 0. Since 0 in Arabic is written as a dot, people draw it at very different sizes, so K-Means split it into small, medium and large dots. The one problem k = 12 could not fix was 6 and 9, which are still mixed together across two clusters."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "537aaf6f-3b74-479e-83ba-a363e532a143",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Before picking a final model, we took the two best models from each clustering type and compared them.\n",
    "\n",
    "**K-Means:** The two best K-Means models were k = 12 (silhouette 0.132) and k = 15 (silhouette 0.133). Their scores are almost the same, and both have balanced clusters, with no cluster holding more than about 13% of the images. k = 15 is only 0.001 higher, but it splits the digits into even more pieces than we need. k = 12 already gives the separate digits their own clusters, so adding more clusters does not help. We did not choose k = 10 because, as shown above, it merged the 3s and 4s.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Hierarchical:** The two best hierarchical models were average linkage (0.140) and ward linkage (0.109). Average linkage has the higher score, but it put 81% of all the images into one cluster, so it is not really separating the digits. Ward has a lower score but much more balanced clusters, so ward was the better of the two.\n",
    "\n",
    "**DBSCAN:** The two best DBSCAN models were eps = 3.5 with min_samples = 20 (0.316) and eps = 3.0 with min_samples = 20 (0.179). These have the highest scores in the whole table, but they are misleading. The first found only 3 clusters, threw out 23.2% of the images as noise and put 73.5% into one cluster. The second found only 4 clusters and threw out 45.4% as noise. Their scores are only high because the silhouette score ignores the points they threw away.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Final choice:** We chose **K-Means with k = 12** (k-means++, n_init = 10, random_state = 0). It has a higher silhouette score than ward (0.132 vs 0.109), it does not throw any images away, its clusters are balanced, and it gives almost every digit its own cluster. The models with higher scores only got them by throwing away data or putting most of the images in one cluster."
   ]
  },
